[Start "1. e4"]

1... a6 ( 1... h6 2. d4 { Black has made a move that does not develop a piece, so take the centre. } 2... g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. Be3 { The same bishop development as against the Pirc, with Qd2 to follow. } ) ( 1... g5 2. d4 { The g5 pawn leaves f5 and h5 weak and Black's bishop will aim at d4. Take the centre. } 2... Bg7 3. Nc3 c6 4. Bxg5 { Grabs the pawn: g5 lost its protector when Black's bishop moved to g7. } 4... Qb6 5. Nf3 ) ( 1... f5 2. exf5 { Takes the pawn. Black has spent a move on a pawn that cannot be held. } 2... Nf6 3. d4 d5 4. Bd3 { Develops and guards the extra pawn on f5. } 4... c5 5. c3 ) 2. d4 { Takes the centre. The a6 move is slow and does nothing about it. } 2... b5 3. Nf3 { Develops and aims at the centre. The bishop will guard e4 next. } 3... Bb7 4. Bd3 { Defends e4 against Black's bishop and eyes the b5 pawn. } 4... Nf6 5. Qe2 { Black's knight and bishop both attack e4, so it needs a second defender. The queen also leaves e5 free for a pawn later. } 5... e6 6. a4 { Hits the b5 pawn, the one that holds Black's queenside together. } 6... c5 7. dxc5 { Plan: take the centre, defend e4, and attack the b5 pawn with a4. Against any other odd first move, play d4, Nc3 or Nf3, and castle. } *
